From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Charlie Shao <charlie.shao@nordicsemi.no>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 11:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(zephyr): break out of the HTTP response wait when the peer
 closes the connection

prv_read_socket_data() treats every non-positive recv() return as
"would block" if errno happens to hold EAGAIN. recv() returns 0 (not
-1) when the peer performs an orderly shutdown, and it leaves errno
untouched in that case -- so a stale EAGAIN, which this thread almost
always carries over from prv_try_send()'s non-blocking send() or from
the TLS handshake's internal socket calls (sockets_tls.c's tls_tx/tls_rx
pass ZSOCK_MSG_DONTWAIT unconditionally), makes the end of the stream
look like a retryable condition. prv_read_socket_data() then reports
success with zero bytes read, memfault_http_parse_response() never
reports the response as complete, and prv_wait_for_http_response()
loops.

The loop does not throttle: after a peer close on a stream socket
Zephyr keeps ZSOCK_POLLIN asserted and adds ZSOCK_POLLHUP
(ztls_poll_update_pollin()), and it deliberately does not reset the
mbedTLS context so that consecutive recv() calls keep returning 0
(recv_tls()). poll() therefore returns immediately every iteration
instead of blocking for POLL_TIMEOUT_MS, and the caller spins.

This is fatal rather than merely slow on this application, because the
periodic-upload work queue runs at K_HIGHEST_APPLICATION_THREAD_PRIO
and holds the project's tls_heap_lock across the post: the spinning
thread outranks every application thread, including the log processing
thread, so UART output stops and no other module can take the TLS lock
again. Recovery requires a power cycle. Observed on v2.6.4.5 as the
device going silent and dropping off Memfault after a random uptime
(~29 min in the reported case, i.e. an arbitrary periodic upload).

Note that an abrupt reset is already handled correctly (POLLERR ->
recv() returns -1 with ECONNRESET -> clean failure); only a graceful
FIN/close_notify hits this path.

Clear errno before recv(), treat 0 as end-of-stream, and bound the
total time spent awaiting a single response as a backstop against any
other state where poll() reports readable indefinitely.

Fixed differently upstream in later SDK versions; this NCS v2.6.4 tree
vendors v1.6.0.
---
 ports/zephyr/common/memfault_platform_http.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ports/zephyr/common/memfault_platform_http.c b/ports/zephyr/common/memfault_platform_http.c
index 4e30692..8a1c3f5 100644
--- a/ports/zephyr/common/memfault_platform_http.c
+++ b/ports/zephyr/common/memfault_platform_http.c
@@ -53,6 +53,10 @@
 // Timeout for the socket file descriptor to become available for I/0
 #define POLL_TIMEOUT_MS 5000
 
+// Upper bound on the total time spent waiting for one HTTP response, as a
+// backstop for any socket state where poll() keeps reporting readable.
+#define RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_MS (4 * POLL_TIMEOUT_MS)
+
 #if (CONFIG_MINIMAL_LIBC_MALLOC_ARENA_SIZE > 0)
 static void *prv_calloc(size_t count, size_t size) {
   return calloc(count, size);
@@ -334,8 +338,19 @@ static int prv_read_socket_data(int sock_fd, void *buf, size_t *buf_len) {
     return false;
   }
 
+  // recv() leaves errno untouched when it returns 0 for an orderly peer
+  // shutdown, so clear it first: otherwise a stale EAGAIN from an earlier
+  // non-blocking send or from the TLS handshake makes end-of-stream look
+  // retryable, and the caller's response loop never terminates.
+  errno = 0;
+
   const int len = recv(sock_fd, buf, *buf_len, MSG_DONTWAIT);
-  if (len <= 0) {
+  if (len == 0) {
+    MEMFAULT_LOG_ERROR("Connection closed by peer while awaiting response");
+    return false;
+  }
+
+  if (len < 0) {
     if ((errno == EAGAIN) || (errno == EWOULDBLOCK)) {
       *buf_len = 0;
       return true;
@@ -350,7 +365,13 @@ static int prv_read_socket_data(int sock_fd, void *buf, size_t *buf_len) {
 
 static bool prv_wait_for_http_response(int sock_fd) {
   sMemfaultHttpResponseContext ctx = { 0 };
+  const int64_t deadline = k_uptime_get() + RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_MS;
+
   while (1) {
+    if (k_uptime_get() > deadline) {
+      MEMFAULT_LOG_ERROR("Timed out awaiting HTTP response");
+      return false;
+    }
     // We don't expect any response that needs to be parsed so
     // just use an arbitrarily small receive buffer
     char buf[32];
-- 
